  "query_preview": "..A dry southwesterly flow remains in place over Utah\nthis evening. Upstream, an upper trough is strengthening off the\nPacNW coast. Meanwhile, Hurricanes Odalys and Polo continue to\nspin in the eastern Pacific.\n\nThe upstream trough will carve south towards the northern/central\nCalifornia coast on Sunday, with southwest flow continuing over \nUtah. Meanwhile, a weak vort lobe originating near Hurricane \nOdalys will become absorbed into the southwesterlies and lift \nacross Utah during the afternoon. This weak disturbance will bring\nincreased moisture and isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms to Utah by Sunday afternoon, generally focused over \nand near the spine of Utah. \n\nAs the trough develops into a closed low over southern California \nand far southern Nevada on Monday, Hurricane Polo will track \nnortheast, moving into Baja California. Moisture from the \nhurricane will become ingested into the southwesterly flow ahead \nof the closed low and increase into southern Utah. This will \ncontinue to bring the potential for widespread and locally heavy \nrain, especially across southern and eastern Utah as the closed \nlow crosses from California into Arizona Monday through Tuesday. \n\nModel QPF continues to creep up for the Monday-Tuesday timeframe,\nespecially notable on the lower end of the probabilistic spectrum\n(NBM 25th percentile values now in excess of 0.6 inches for the \nbulk of south-central and southeast Utah), which increases \nconfidence in wet conditions. A good portion of south-central \nthrough southeast Utah has a 60-70% chance of exceeding 1 inch \nMonday through Tuesday, highest along the Arizona border. \nPrecipitation amounts will decrease farther north and east into \nUtah, with east-central Utah having a 60-70% chance of exceeding \n0.5 inches, and areas farther north and east with a 30% chance or \nless.\n\nThe heavy rain will bring an increased risk of flooding/flash \nflooding to the area. The WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook now has \na Moderate Risk across portions of southern Utah, and the Slight \nRisk now extends into central and portions of northeast Utah. \nAlthough model instability is increasing, especially across \nsouthwest Utah, precipitation may well end up as largely \nstratiform with embedded convective elements, and the strength and\ncoverage of these convective elements will most likely determine \nthe extent of any significant flash flooding.\n\nCooler temperatures will spread into the area Monday and Tuesday,\nwith afternoon maxes falling to around 4-10 degrees below normal\nand largely failing to rise above the low to mid 70s in most \nvalleys. Temperatures will slowly rebound midweek as the storm\nsystem exits the area. There is still some model spread with\nregards to the large-scale pattern for the latter part of the\nweek. Some models have been trying to bring a trough back into the\narea, but based on cluster analysis of global ensembles, it\nappears that any trough may end up being relatively weak.",
